window.docContents[51].resolve({"/UsingLean/#Logical-Foundations--UsingLean___-Using-the-Full-Power-of-a-Proof-Assistant--Definitional-Simplification-with--dsimp":{"contents":"We will drop dsimp from here and discuss it in Lists instead, using a NatProd\nexample to motivate it. The use of the lambda term here is confusing.\n\nSometimes when you unfold a definition your hypothesis or goal may become hard to understand.\nWhen that happens, it can be useful to simplify it.\nTo apply simplifications similar to those that rfl does,\nbut without also trying to close an equality goal,\nyou can use the tactic dsimp only or dsimp only at h.\n\n\n\nexample : (fun x => x + 0) n = n := by\n  dsimp only -- applies the function to its argument\n  rw [Nat.add_zero]\n\n\nIf we did not simplify here before attempting to rewrite, we would get an error:\n\nexample : (fun x => x + 0) n = n := by\n  rw [Nat.add_zero]\n\n\nTactic `rewrite` failed: Did not find an occurrence of the pattern\n  ?n + 0\nin the target expression\n  (fun x => x + 0) n = n\n\nn : Nat\n⊢ (fun x => x + 0) n = n\n\n\n","context":"Logical Foundations\u0009UsingLean: Using the Full Power of a Proof Assistant","header":"4.5. Definitional Simplification with  dsimp","id":"/UsingLean/#Logical-Foundations--UsingLean___-Using-the-Full-Power-of-a-Proof-Assistant--Definitional-Simplification-with--dsimp"}});